# Security, Data Protection & Sensitive-Data Handling

## Data Protection (applicable data-protection law, GDPR-class)
- Collect minimum necessary personal data. Document purpose for each field.
- Provide data deletion mechanism for users (right to erasure).
- Provide a self-service data export (right of access/portability), with sensitive fields redacted per the rules below.
- Define a retention period per personal-data category and enforce it (TTL / scheduled purge).
- Consent must be explicit — no pre-checked boxes.
- Personal data stored in the relational DB must be identifiable for deletion requests.

## Sensitive Data Handling
- **`<national-id>`**: store as SHA-256 hash only (hashed client-side; `national_id_hash`). Never
  plaintext, never reversible, never in logs.
- **`<tax-id>`**: store as a HYBRID pair, never as plaintext in a queryable column (Phase 16.33):
  - `tax_id_hash` — deterministic SHA-256 hex (64 lowercase) for the UNIQUE constraint + admin
    exact-match search. Satisfies the "hashed" requirement; treat as sensitive (low-entropy,
    enumerable) — never expose publicly.
  - `tax_id_encrypted` — KMS envelope ciphertext (`src/shared/crypto/tax-id.ts` on the shared
    field-encryption CMK), reversible ONLY by roles holding `kms:Decrypt` on that CMK. This is
    a deliberate, documented deviation from "hash only" so **admin identity verification can
    read the value back** to reconcile it against uploaded documents. Read is restricted to the
    owning party (self) + the admin verification service + the document processor (OCR
    cross-check). Never in logs; never in the self-service data export (redacted there — the
    admin verification flow is the reveal path).
    A future hardening phase may upgrade `tax_id_hash` to a KMS-keyed HMAC if enumeration risk
    warrants (the 64-hex column shape is compatible).
- **Phone numbers**: displayed **masked** (e.g. `<country-code> ****XX78`, shared `maskPhone()`)
  in any surface a non-authorized caller can reach; authorized staff (admin/operator) and the
  owner see the full number. At-rest phone encryption is a tracked future hardening item
  (deferred — no plaintext phone in logs meanwhile).
- **Party documents**: stored in S3 private bucket, accessed via time-limited pre-signed URLs (15 min max).
- **API keys / secrets**: AWS Secrets Manager or SSM Parameter Store. Never in code, env files, or git.

## Input Validation & Sanitization
- Validate all inputs server-side with Zod schemas, even if client-side validation exists.
- Sanitize HTML content with DOMPurify before rendering user-generated content.
- Rate-limit all public endpoints (API Gateway throttling + WAF rules).

## Authentication Security
- Cognito PKCE flow — no implicit grant.
- Access tokens: 1 hour expiry. Refresh tokens: 30 days.
- Force MFA for admin and internal-operator roles.
- Lock accounts after 5 failed login attempts (Cognito built-in).

## Security Testing — probe correctness (so a green/red result means something)
- **Never mutate the TRAILING character of a base64url payload to prove tamper-rejection.** For a
  fixed-length binary (a JWT signature, hash, or MAC), the last base64url char carries mostly
  **padding bits**, so flipping it (e.g. `A→B`) decodes to **byte-identical** bytes a predictable
  fraction of the time — the "tampered" token still verifies and the probe FALSE-passes/fails
  intermittently (F-N5: a Cognito RS256/RSA-2048 sig is 256 B → 342 base64url chars whose final
  char has only 2 significant + 4 padding bits; the `A→B` flip was a no-op ~25% of nights → a
  phantom S0 "auth bypass"). Instead tamper an **interior** char (always 6 significant bits) or
  **decode → flip a byte → re-encode**. Prove the mutation actually changes the decoded bytes.
- **An intermittently passing/failing security test is itself a defect** — treat it as a bug in the
  probe or the system, never "flaky, re-run" (see `systematic-debugging.mdc`). Verify a probe's
  verdict by its real effect, not its printed label.

## Authorization (Lambda RBAC)
- Every API handler checks user role before executing business logic.
- Data-level filtering: queries add `WHERE` clauses based on role.
- Owner-scoped access: a non-admin caller can only see/modify records it owns — scope every query by the caller's own id/tenant from the token, never from request input.
- Public (unauthenticated) endpoints return only what the public surface needs — never individual private records.

